course: language
lesson: 2
title: Nouns: the names of things, people, places and ideas
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A noun is a naming word: it names a person (girl, Mia), a place (kitchen, London), a thing (cat, mat) or an idea or feeling (happiness, fear).
explain: In "The cat sat on the mat in the kitchen" there are three nouns: cat, mat and kitchen; "the" is a pointer word, "sat" is the doing word, and "on" and "in" show where.
explain: A good test: can you put "the" or "some" in front of it, and does it name something? "The happiness", "some fear" work, so happiness and fear are nouns, even though you can't touch them.
explain: The same word can change jobs: in "I run fast" run is a verb, but in "The run was long" it is a noun, because now it names a thing.
explain: English keeps making new nouns: spork (spoon + fork), brunch (breakfast + lunch) and smog (smoke + fog) were made by squashing two old words together, and places have their own nouns, like "butty" for a sandwich in Manchester.
fact: A noun names a person, place, thing or idea.
fact: A determiner is a word like the, a, this or my that comes before a noun.
fact: A concrete noun names something you can see or touch, like a mat; an abstract noun names an idea or feeling, like happiness, fear or friendship.
fact: A proper noun is the name of a particular person, place, day or month, like Mia, London or Monday, and starts with a capital letter; a common noun is a general word like girl or city.
fact: The word "I" always has a capital letter in English.
fact: A collective noun names a group, like a team, a class, a herd or a flock of sheep.
fact: Many fancy animal group names, like a murder of crows or a parliament of owls, come from old hunting lists written in the 1400s and are used mostly for fun.
fact: A word's class (noun, verb, adjective and so on) depends on the job it does in the sentence.
fact: Countable nouns can be counted (sweets); uncountable nouns are measured (sugar, water). In careful writing people use "fewer" with countable nouns and "less" with uncountable ones, though in casual speech many say "less" for both.
fact: Some nouns end in s but take a singular verb, like "the news is" and "maths is"; some are always plural, like trousers, scissors and glasses, because they have two parts.
fact: With group nouns like team or family, British English often says "the team are" and American English usually says "the team is"; both are correct in their own country.
fact: A blend is a new word made from parts of two words, like spork, brunch and smog.
fact: In north-west England a butty is a sandwich, and "our kid" means my brother or sister, or a close mate.
exercise: Which words are the nouns in "The cat sat on the mat in the kitchen"? | cat, mat, kitchen | cat, sat, mat, kitchen | the, cat, mat | sat, on, in
exercise: In that sentence, what kind of word is "the"? | A determiner | A noun | A verb | A preposition
exercise: Ethan says "happiness" can't be a noun because you can't touch it. Is he right? | No, it is an abstract noun that names a feeling | Yes, happiness is a verb, because you do being happy | Yes, happiness is a verb | No, it is a proper noun
exercise: Which of these is an abstract noun? | friendship | table | puppy | river
exercise: Lily writes "i went to london with mia on monday". Which words need capitals? | i, london, mia, monday | only the first word, i | london and mia only | i, went, london, monday
exercise: What is a proper noun? | The name of a particular person, place, day or month | A noun that is very polite and used only with grown-ups | A noun that is very polite | A noun that comes first in a sentence
exercise: What is a collective noun? | A word for a group, like herd or swarm | A noun with a capital letter, like London or Monday | A noun with a capital letter | A noun made from two words
exercise: Where do names like "a murder of crows" come from? | Old hunting lists written in the 1400s, kept mostly for fun | Crows really are known to murder each other | Farmers and park keepers use these names every single day | Farmers use these names every day
exercise: In "The run was long", what is "run"? | A noun | An action verb | An adjective | A preposition
exercise: How do you decide a word's class? | Look at the job it does in this sentence | Look only at its last letter and its first letter | Look only at its last letter | Check how long the word is
exercise: In careful writing, which is right? | fewer sweets and less sugar | less sweets and fewer sugar | fewer sweets and fewer sugar | less sweets and less sugars
exercise: Which is correct? | The news is good | The news are good | The newses are good | The new's are good
exercise: Why do we say "the trousers are" for one pair? | Trousers have two legs, so the word is always plural | Trousers were once always sold in shops in pairs of two | Trousers were once sold in pairs of two | It is only plural in America
exercise: Sophie writes "Our team are winning". Her American pen pal says it's wrong. Who is right? | Both: British English often says "are", American English "is" | Only Sophie, because a team has many players so it must be are | Only Sophie, "team" must take "are" | Neither, it should be "Our team am winning"
exercise: Mo's word "spork" joins spoon and fork. What is that kind of word? | A blend | A proper noun | A collective noun | A plural noun
exercise: Jade's dad says "Get your butties, our kid." What is a butty? | A sandwich | A warm coat | A school shoe | A bus ticket
exercise: How many nouns are in "My sister's happiness filled the house on Sunday"? | Four: sister's, happiness, house, Sunday | Three: house, Sunday, sister's | Two: house and Sunday | Five: my, sister's, happiness, house, Sunday
exercise: In "The cook will cook the fish that they fish for", how many times is a word used as a noun? | Two: the cook and the fish | Four: every cook and every fish | Three: cook, fish and they | One: only the first cook
test: Your mate goes "fear isn't a noun bro, you can't pick it up." What's the best reply? | It names a feeling, so it is an abstract noun | He's right, nouns must be solid things you can pick up | Fear is only a verb, like "I fear" | Fear is a proper noun with a capital F
test: Nan texts "meet u in leeds on friday x". If you wrote it up properly for school, which words would you capitalise? | Leeds and Friday | meet and leeds | Friday only | none, texts and school are the same
test: A lad from Liverpool says "our kid's sound, he made me a chip butty." ("Sound" means nice, decent.) What happened? | His brother, a nice lad, made him a chip sandwich | His friend made a loud noise with chips | His kid made him a chip shop | His brother sold him some chips from a sandwich shop
test: Your sister rolls her eyes: "less sweets, fewer sweets, who cares, innit?" What's the fair answer? | In school writing say fewer for things you count, and don't bully people over it | It's a terrible mistake that makes you sound stupid, so always correct people loudly | Less is always right and fewer is old-fashioned | Fewer is only used for money
test: What would happen if a word could never change its class? | "Run" couldn't be both a thing and an action, so we'd need more words | Nothing at all would change in English | All nouns would turn into verbs | Sentences would lose their capital letters
test: A cartoon narrator says "Look, a murder of crows!" and everyone in the park nods. Is that how people really talk? | No, it's an old fun name; most people just say "a lot of crows" | Yes, everyone uses it every day | Yes, it is the only correct word | No, the right name is "a flock of murders", as every bird book says
test: A Gen Alpha kid says "new words? no cap, that's sus, all words are mega old." ("Sus" means suspicious, doubtful.) What's true? | New words are made all the time, like spork and brunch | He's right, nobody can make new words | Only teachers are allowed to make new words | New words only come into English from other languages now
test: Rory says "maths ends in s, so it's 'maths are hard'." What's the right reply? | Maths takes a singular verb: "maths is hard" | He's right, every word ending in s is plural | It should be "math are hard" | Maths has no verb at all
test: An American gran says "Your family are lovely" sounds weird. Is the British version wrong? | No, both forms are correct in their own country | Yes, British English is always wrong | Yes, family can never take "are" | No, but only in poems
test: Grace says "scissors is sharp". Which is the usual form? | The scissors are sharp | The scissor is sharps | The scissors is sharp | The scissor are sharp
test: Is "smog" a noun? | Yes, a blend of smoke and fog | No, it is a verb meaning to smoke | No, it is a made-up spelling mistake | Yes, a proper noun, the name of a city
exam: Select the response that correctly enumerates the nouns contained in the sentence "The cat sat on the mat in the kitchen". | cat, mat and kitchen | the, sat and on | cat, sat and in | mat, on and the
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an abstract noun? | A noun designating an idea or emotion not perceptible by touch | A noun designating a particular person or locality within a nation | A noun designating a particular person or locality | A noun employed exclusively in scientific discourse
exam: Pursuant to the conventions of careful written English, identify the correctly formulated expression. | Fewer sweets and less sugar | Less sweets and fewer sugar | Fewer sugar and fewer sweets | Less sweets and less sugars
exam: In respect of the expressions "the team are" and "the team is", which statement is consistent with the established facts? | The former is common in British usage and the latter in American usage, and both are acceptable | The former is invariably erroneous in every variety of English | The latter is invariably erroneous in every variety of English | Both expressions are erroneous and ought to be avoided
